# V850E1F floating-point unit (mnemonic set and encodings per the IDA
# nec850 E1F block, ref. U16374EJ1V0UM). The E1F FPU operates on the
# general-purpose registers as single-precision values and adds two FPU
# registers: EFG (floating-point arithmetic FLAG register) and ECT
# (floating-point arithmetic CONTROL register).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contract classification. Every semantic below is labeled, and every
# contract that is not recoverable from a primary source is represented
# ONLY as an explicit unresolved-E1F intrinsic (a user pcodeop), so the
# unknown vendor behavior stays opaque to analysis (the same convention
# this module already uses for interrupt-control instructions and the
# AssertProcessorFixtureSemantics stepInterruptControl pattern) instead
# of being invented as dataflow. Nothing guessed is asserted by
# AssertV850E1f.java.
#
# [D] Documented, PHO3 R01UH0128ED0730 Rev.7.30 (V850E1 core device):
#     - pp.104-105 Tables 3-10..3-12: EFG bits RO13 IV12 ZD11 VF10 UD9
#       PR8 TR4 OV2 S1 Z0, all other bits fixed at 0, reset value 0;
#       ECT bits IT12 ZT11 VT10 UT9 PT8, others fixed 0, reset 0;
#       ECT only gates TR ("Each bit of ECT is a mask bit for one
#       condition"; with the mask set a detection sets the cause bit AND
#       TR, without it only the cause bit); TR "summarizes the state of
#       the FPU: 1: Abnormal condition detected: one of the bits 12 to 8
#       of the ECT register is set"; OV = overflow "during
#       floating-point to integer conversion"; S/Z = operation result
#       negative/zero; RO: 0 = operation in progress, 1 = FPU idle.
#     - CPU overview: the FPU is "single precision, according to IEEE
#       754-85 standard".
#     - pp.98-99 Table 3-5: V850E1 PSW condition bits Z0 S1 OV2 CY3
#       SAT4 (context only; TRFF's mapping is unresolved).
#     - Reset table: EFG = ECT = 0.
#     - R01US0001EJ0100 Appendix E: V850E1 provides no floating-point
#       exception; flags are consumed only by software polling.
# [I] IEEE 754-1985-mandated, given PHO3's conformance statement, and
#     implemented as real p-code: correctly rounded add/sub/mul/div
#     results (round to nearest, ties to even - the standard's default
#     and the only mode EFG/ECT exposes no control for); the five
#     exception CONDITIONS (invalid operation incl. sNaN operands,
#     0*inf, inf-inf, 0/0, inf/inf; divide by zero for finite nonzero
#     dividend; overflow; underflow as tininess + inexact, with the
#     standard permitting pre/post-rounding tininess detection - this
#     model detects post-rounding; inexactness); +-infinity RESULTS on
#     overflow and divide-by-zero; gradual underflow (subnormals);
#     float->int conversion rounding of in-range values. These
#     conditions are computed exactly and fed to the unresolved status
#     intrinsic below; only their DETECTION is claimed, not their
#     retention or visibility rules.
# [U] UNRESOLVED, represented exclusively by intrinsics. The exact
#     vendor contracts live in the FPU volume of the V850E1 core
#     architecture manual - MISSING PREREQUISITE, exact and named:
#     "V850E1 32-Bit Microprocessor Core Architecture (Floating Point
#     Operation Unit) (User's Manual)" U16374EJ1V0UM00, November 2008
#     (base volume: U14559EJ3V1UM00, February 2004); both numbers and
#     titles are quoted by Renesas R01TU0011ED0100 V850E/PHO3 Operating
#     Precautions, p.20 "(C) Valid Specification". Confirmed NOT
#     publicly retrievable (renesas.com legacy paths, Wayback CDX across
#     the Renesas/NEC doc hosts, archive.org incl. bitsavers NEC,
#     alldatasheet [all V850E1 items are the base volume U14559EJ3V1UM],
#     every mirror the source scout could reach); no open implementation
#     (binutils-gdb sim, GCC, rizin, radare2) implements the E1F FPU;
#     IDA decodes it citing U16374EJ1V0UM without flag semantics.
#       e1f_status_unresolved(EFG, cause, result) -> EFG
#         how an instruction's detected conditions (cause: OV=bit5,
#         IV=bit4, ZD=bit3, VF=bit2, UD=bit1, PR=bit0), the operation
#         result sign/zero, and RO are merged into EFG: the per-op
#         update and retention rules are unknown.
#       e1f_trff_unresolved(PSW, EFG) -> PSW
#         which EFG bits TRFF publishes into which PSW bits (the EFG
#         Z/S/OV/flag positions 0/1/2/4 numerically coincide with the
#         PSW Z/S/OV/SAT positions of Table 3-5, but that alignment
#         proves no transfer contract).
#       e1f_cvt_range_unresolved(src, mode) -> word
#         the result value of a float->int conversion whose magnitude
#         overflows int32 (or whose input is NaN); no family table is
#         E1F authority, so no saturation value is invented.
#       e1f_minmax_nan_unresolved(a, b, pick) -> word
#         the result of minf.s/maxf.s when an operand is NaN.
#     Modeled value (documented boundary, module convention of
#     v850_fused.sinc): invalid arithmetic produces the canonical quiet
#     NaN 0x7fc00000 - IEEE 754-1985 requires a NaN result but leaves
#     the payload to the implementation; the payload is not silicon
#     truth and fixtures assert only NaN-ness. LDFF/LDFC keep only the
#     defined register bits (a reserved bit is physically absent) and
#     the documented TR derivation then runs on the merged value.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

define pcodeop e1f_status_unresolved;
define pcodeop e1f_trff_unresolved;
define pcodeop e1f_cvt_range_unresolved;
define pcodeop e1f_minmax_nan_unresolved;

# ---- EFG/ECT bit positions (PHO3 Table 3-11 / Table 3-12) [D] ----
@define E1F_RO 0x2000
@define E1F_IV 0x1000
@define E1F_ZD 0x0800
@define E1F_VF 0x0400
@define E1F_UD 0x0200
@define E1F_PR 0x0100
@define E1F_TR 0x0010
@define E1F_OV 0x0004
@define E1F_S  0x0002
@define E1F_Z  0x0001
@define E1F_EFG_BITS 0x3F17	# RO..PR, TR, OV, S, Z; all else fixed 0 [D]
@define E1F_ECT_BITS 0x1F00	# IT..PT; all else fixed 0 [D]

# TR is a derived state bit: the OR of every detected condition whose ECT
# mask is set (PHO3 p.105) [D]. Recomputed after every EFG/ECT write, so
# loading a new mask (LDFC) or new flags (LDFF) updates TR immediately.
# This derivation is real p-code and runs on whatever EFG value the
# (unresolved) producer left; the fixture exercises it through LDFF/LDFC,
# which are fully real.
macro e1f_refresh_tr() {
	local hit:4 = EFG & ECT;	# cause bits 12..8 align with masks 12..8 [D]
	EFG = (EFG & ~$(E1F_TR)) | (zext(hit != 0) << 4);
}

# Hand an instruction's computed conditions and result to the unresolved
# EFG merge [U]. The inputs are real dataflow (the decompiler sees EFG
# depend on the operands); the update/retention rule stays opaque, and
# the documented TR derivation runs after it in dataflow order.
macro e1f_status(cause, ov, result) {
	local conditions:4 = cause;
	local overflow:1 = ov;
	EFG = e1f_status_unresolved(EFG,
		(conditions & 0x1f) | (zext(overflow != 0) << 5), result);
	e1f_refresh_tr();
}

# Single-precision operation with exact exception-condition inputs [I].
# kind: 0=add 1=sub 2=mul 3=div; result = src2 op src1; the result value
# is real IEEE p-code.
# Condition exactness: sums/products of binary32 operands are exact in
# binary64 whenever the operand exponent-field gap is <= 29 (<= 53
# significant bits), and a gap > 29 can only round to the larger
# operand, so the dropped operand alone decides inexactness; a 24x24-bit
# significand product is always exact in binary64; division is exact iff
# the odd part of the divisor significand divides the dividend
# significand (the quotient is then a <=24-bit significand times a power
# of two).
macro e1f_arith(dest, src1, src2, kind) {
	local a:4 = src1;
	local b:4 = src2;
	local action:4 = kind;
	local aa:4;
	local bb:4;
	local cause:4 = 0;
	local r:4 = 0;
	local a8:8;
	local b8:8;
	local d8:8;
	local pr:1 = 0;
	local ea:4;
	local eb:4;
	local md:8;
	local mn:8;
	if (action != 1) goto <ready>;
	a = a ^ 0x80000000;		# subtraction is addition of the negated subtrahend
	<ready>
	aa = a & 0x7fffffff;
	bb = b & 0x7fffffff;
	a8 = float2float(a);
	b8 = float2float(b);
	# IEEE 754-1985 7.1: a signaling NaN operand is an invalid operation;
	# a quiet NaN operand propagates without a flag. [I]
	if ((aa > 0x7f800000) && ((aa & 0x400000) == 0)) goto <invalid>;
	if ((bb > 0x7f800000) && ((bb & 0x400000) == 0)) goto <invalid>;
	if ((aa > 0x7f800000) || (bb > 0x7f800000)) goto <nan_result>;
	if (action == 3) goto <division>;
	if (action == 2) goto <multiply>;
	# ---- addition (subtraction via the sign flip above) ----
	if ((aa == 0x7f800000) || (bb == 0x7f800000)) goto <add_infinite>;
	r = b f+ a;
	d8 = b8 f+ a8;
	pr = float2float(r) f!= d8;
	ea = aa >> 23;
	eb = bb >> 23;
	if (ea >= eb) goto <a_wide>;
	if (((eb - ea) > 29) && (aa != 0)) goto <dropped>;
	goto <round_flags>;
	<a_wide>
	if (((ea - eb) > 29) && (bb != 0)) goto <dropped>;
	goto <round_flags>;
	# ---- multiplication ----
	<multiply>
	if ((aa == 0x7f800000) || (bb == 0x7f800000)) goto <mul_infinite>;
	r = b f* a;
	d8 = b8 f* a8;			# 24x24-bit significands: exact in binary64
	pr = float2float(r) f!= d8;
	goto <round_flags>;
	# ---- division: result = b / a ----
	<division>
	if ((aa == 0x7f800000) && (bb == 0x7f800000)) goto <invalid>;
	if (aa == 0x7f800000) goto <div_by_inf>;
	if (bb == 0x7f800000) goto <inf_div>;
	if (aa == 0) goto <div_by_zero>;
	r = b f/ a;
	d8 = b8 f/ a8;
	pr = float2float(r) f!= d8;
	md = zext(aa & 0x7fffff) | (zext(aa >= 0x00800000) << 23);
	if ((md & 1) != 0) goto <md_odd>;
	<md_strip>				# isolate the odd part of the divisor
	md = md >> 1;
	if ((md & 1) == 0) goto <md_strip>;
	<md_odd>
	mn = zext(bb & 0x7fffff) | (zext(bb >= 0x00800000) << 23);
	if ((mn % md) == 0) goto <round_flags>;
	goto <dropped>;
	# ---- IEEE special combinations [I] ----
	<div_by_zero>			# 0/0 is invalid; x/0 sets ZD and returns +-inf
	if (bb == 0) goto <invalid>;
	cause = cause | 0x08;
	r = ((b ^ a) & 0x80000000) | 0x7f800000;
	goto <finish>;
	<div_by_inf>			# finite (or zero) / infinity: exact signed zero
	r = (b ^ a) & 0x80000000;
	goto <finish>;
	<inf_div>				# infinity / finite (or zero): +-inf, no flag
	r = ((b ^ a) & 0x80000000) | 0x7f800000;
	goto <finish>;
	<mul_infinite>			# 0 * infinity is invalid
	if ((aa == 0) || (bb == 0)) goto <invalid>;
	r = ((b ^ a) & 0x80000000) | 0x7f800000;
	goto <finish>;
	<add_infinite>
	if ((aa == 0x7f800000) && (bb == 0x7f800000)) goto <add_inf_inf>;
	if (aa == 0x7f800000) goto <take_a>;
	r = b;					# b is +-inf: adding finite a preserves it
	goto <finish>;
	<take_a>
	r = a;
	goto <finish>;
	<add_inf_inf>			# same-sign infinities add; opposite signs are invalid
	if (((a ^ b) & 0x80000000) != 0) goto <invalid>;
	r = a;
	goto <finish>;
	<invalid>
	cause = 0x10;			# IV [I]
	<nan_result>
	r = 0x7fc00000;			# NaN result is IEEE-mandated; payload is a
	goto <finish>;			# documented model value, not silicon truth
	# ---- rounding-driven conditions [I] ----
	<dropped>
	pr = 1;					# bits below the binary32 window exist
	<round_flags>
	# Overflow: the rounded magnitude exceeds the largest finite binary32
	# (post-rounding detection, permitted by IEEE 754-1985 7.3); overflow
	# is always inexact, so VF comes with PR.
	if ((r & 0x7fffffff) == 0x7f800000) goto <overflowed>;
	# Tininess: the rounded result is below the normal range; the UD flag
	# additionally requires loss of accuracy (IEEE 754-1985 7.5).
	if ((r & 0x7fffffff) >= 0x00800000) goto <flags_ready>;
	cause = cause | (zext(pr != 0) << 1);
	goto <flags_ready>;
	<overflowed>
	cause = cause | 0x05;	# VF | PR
	<flags_ready>
	cause = cause | zext(pr != 0);	# PR
	<finish>
	dest = r;				# the IEEE result write happens before the
	e1f_status(cause, 0, r);# unresolved status boundary [U]
}

# Float -> signed word conversion. mode 0 = cvt.sw (round to nearest -
# the decode-name convention; every nearest variant agrees on the values
# the fixture uses), mode 1 = trnc.sw (round toward zero). In-range
# magnitudes convert with real p-code [I]; the documented OV condition
# (PHO3 Table 3-11 bit 2) is computed as an input to the unresolved
# status merge; the RESULT VALUE of an overflowing (or NaN) conversion
# is the e1f_cvt_range_unresolved intrinsic [U] - no family saturation
# endpoint is invented.
macro e1f_cvt_sw(dest, src, mode) {
	local conversion_mode:4 = mode;
	local x:4 = src;
	local xx:4 = x & 0x7fffffff;
	local fexp:4 = xx >> 23;
	local mag:8;
	local low:8;
	local half:8;
	local k:4;
	local cause:4 = 0;
	local ov:1 = 0;
	local r:4 = 0;
	if (xx <= 0x7f800000) goto <finite>;
	cause = 0x10;			# IV: NaN converts as an invalid operation [I condition]
	r = e1f_cvt_range_unresolved(x, conversion_mode);
	goto <finish>;
	<finite>
	if (xx >= 0x00800000) goto <normal>;
	r = x & 0x80000000;		# |x| < 2^-126 rounds to signed zero
	if (xx == 0) goto <finish>;
	cause = 0x01;			# PR: a nonzero fraction is always lost
	goto <finish>;
	<normal>
	mag = zext((xx & 0x7fffff) | 0x800000);
	if (fexp < 126) goto <rounds_to_zero>;
	if (fexp > 189) goto <out_of_range>;
	if (fexp >= 151) goto <wide>;
	k = 150 - fexp;		# fraction bits below the integer part: 0..24
	if (k == 0) goto <ranged>;
	low = mag & ((1:8 << k) - 1);
	mag = mag >> k;
	cause = cause | zext(low != 0);	# a lost fraction is inexact
	if (conversion_mode != 0) goto <ranged>;	# truncate: magnitude kept as-is
	half = 1:8 << (k - 1);				# nearest, ties to even [I]
	if (low < half) goto <ranged>;
	mag = mag + zext((low > half) || ((mag & 1) != 0));
	goto <ranged>;
	<wide>					# integer with trailing zero bits: field 151..189
	mag = mag << (fexp - 150);
	goto <ranged>;
	<rounds_to_zero>
	r = x & 0x80000000;
	cause = cause | 0x01;
	goto <finish>;
	# ---- range check and signed assembly ----
	<ranged>
	if (mag <= 0x7fffffff) goto <fits>;
	if (mag != 0x80000000) goto <out_of_range>;
	if ((x & 0x80000000) == 0) goto <out_of_range>;
	r = 0x80000000;			# -2^31 is exactly representable [I]
	goto <finish>;
	<out_of_range>
	ov = 1;					# OV: conversion overflow [D condition]
	r = e1f_cvt_range_unresolved(x, conversion_mode);
	goto <finish>;
	<fits>
	r = mag:4;
	if ((x & 0x80000000) == 0) goto <finish>;
	r = 0 - r;
	<finish>
	dest = r;
	e1f_status(cause, ov, r);
}

# Signed word -> float conversion: IEEE round-to-nearest with real
# p-code [I]; the PR input for a lost fraction and the merge remain
# unresolved [U].
macro e1f_cvt_ws(dest, src) {
	local r:4 = int2float(src);
	local cause:4 = 0;
	local wide:8 = int2float(src);	# int32 -> binary64 is exact
	cause = zext(float2float(r) f!= wide);
	dest = r;
	e1f_status(cause, 0, r);
}

# minf.s/maxf.s numeric select: numeric operands select with a strict
# `f<` (decode-name semantics); a NaN operand routes the RESULT through
# e1f_minmax_nan_unresolved [U]. The status update is unresolved [U].
macro e1f_minmax(dest, src1, src2, pick_max) {
	local a:4 = src1;
	local b:4 = src2;
	local action:4 = pick_max;
	local r:4;
	if (((a & 0x7fffffff) > 0x7f800000)
		|| ((b & 0x7fffffff) > 0x7f800000)) goto <nan>;
	r = a;
	if (action != 0) goto <do_max>;
	if (!(b f< a)) goto <publish>;
	r = b;		# min: ties keep reg1's value
	goto <publish>;
	<do_max>
	if (!(a f< b)) goto <publish>;
	r = b;		# max: ties keep reg1's value
	goto <publish>;
	<nan>
	r = e1f_minmax_nan_unresolved(a, b, action);
	<publish>
	dest = r;
	e1f_status(0, 0, r);
}

# Format F:I reg1, reg2, reg3: reg3 = reg2 op reg1 (single precision).
:divf.s R0004, R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R0004 & R1115; op1626=0x3E0 & R2731 {
	e1f_arith(R2731, R0004, R1115, 3);
}

:subf.s R0004, R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R0004 & R1115; op1626=0x3E4 & R2731 {
	e1f_arith(R2731, R0004, R1115, 1);
}

:addf.s R0004, R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R0004 & R1115; op1626=0x3E8 & R2731 {
	e1f_arith(R2731, R0004, R1115, 0);
}

:mulf.s R0004, R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R0004 & R1115; op1626=0x3EC & R2731 {
	e1f_arith(R2731, R0004, R1115, 2);
}

:minf.s R0004, R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R0004 & R1115; op1626=0x3F0 & R2731 {
	e1f_minmax(R2731, R0004, R1115, 0);
}

:maxf.s R0004, R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R0004 & R1115; op1626=0x3F4 & R2731 {
	e1f_minmax(R2731, R0004, R1115, 1);
}

# Format F:II reg2, reg3.
:cvt.sw R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R1115; op1626=0x360 & R2731 {
	e1f_cvt_sw(R2731, R1115, 0);
}

:trnc.sw R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R1115; op1626=0x368 & R2731 {
	e1f_cvt_sw(R2731, R1115, 1);
}

:cvt.ws R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R1115; op1626=0x370 & R2731 {
	e1f_cvt_ws(R2731, R1115);
}

# negf/absf are pure sign-bit operations on the value; their EFG effect
# (if any) is unresolved [U].
:negf.s R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R1115; op1626=0x3F8 & R2731 {
	R2731 = R1115 ^ 0x80000000;
	e1f_status(0, 0, R2731);
}

:absf.s R1115, R2731 is op0510=0x3F & R1115; op1626=0x3FC & R2731 {
	R2731 = R1115 & 0x7fffffff;
	e1f_status(0, 0, R2731);
}

# Format F:IV: FPU register transfers. STFF/LDFF move EFG, STFC/LDFC
# move ECT, all real p-code: reserved bits of both registers are
# physically absent (PHO3 pp.104-105 diagrams) [D], so loads keep only
# the defined bits, and the documented TR derivation then runs [D].
# Whether software can set the cause bits through LDFF, and what LDFF
# does to RO, is part of the unresolved status contract [U].
:stff "efg", R2731 is op0510=0x3F; op1626=0x378 & R2731 & op2731!=0 {
	R2731 = EFG;
}

# TRFF publishes the EFG condition flags into the PSW [decode name];
# which bits go where is exactly the unresolved contract of
# e1f_trff_unresolved [U]. NOTE: the E1F encoding overlaps the RH850
# LDL space, so the E1F language must be selected to decode it.
:trff is op0510=0x3F; op1626=0x378 & op2731=0 {
	PSW = e1f_trff_unresolved(PSW, EFG);
}

:stfc "ect", R2731 is op0510=0x3F; op1626=0x37C & R2731 {
	R2731 = ECT;
}

:ldff R1115, "efg" is op0510=0x3F & R1115; op1626=0x37A & op2731=0 {
	EFG = R1115 & $(E1F_EFG_BITS);
	e1f_refresh_tr();
}

:ldfc R1115, "ect" is op0510=0x3F & R1115; op1626=0x37E & op2731=0 {
	ECT = R1115 & $(E1F_ECT_BITS);
	e1f_refresh_tr();
}
